        <div id="propane-only" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Propane-Only vs Dual Fuel: What Is Actually For Sale
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Few Propane-Only Portables Remain</h3>

          <p>
            Search for a propane powered generator and you will find surprisingly few that run on propane alone. Generac once sold propane-only portables under its LP series, and those are now discontinued. Sun Joe sold the SJG4100LP, a 4,100 starting and 3,300 running watt propane-only portable, but when we checked, both its Amazon listing and Sun Joe's own store showed it as unavailable. Small specialist brands sell 1,000 watt propane-only units, but we could not find them listed on Amazon US. The market has moved almost entirely to dual fuel, and more recently tri fuel.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Dual Fuel Is Not a Loss</h3>

          <p>
            That is not entirely a loss. A dual fuel generator runs on propane just as a propane-only unit does, using a regulator and a fuel selector, and it can also switch to gasoline when a cylinder freezes up, runs out at 2 a.m., or when propane is simply not available. What you give up is a little simplicity and, on some models, the clean carburettor that a never-gasoline engine keeps. If you never put gasoline in a dual fuel unit, you largely keep that benefit too.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How We Treated Dual Fuel Picks</h3>

          <p>
            So every pick on this page is a dual fuel generator, and we have said so on each one. We chose them on propane performance, not gasoline: published propane running watts, how that output maps to real loads, propane runtime and how each behaves as a propane-first machine. That is the difference between this guide and our <a href="/best-dual-fuel-generator/">best dual fuel generator</a> roundup, which ranks machines mainly on gasoline output for whole-home backup, and our <a href="/best-tri-fuel-generator/">best tri-fuel generator</a> guide, which is for homes that can connect to a natural gas line.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check Propane-Only Claims</h3>

          <p>
            If you see a listing claiming to be a propane-only generator, check the fuel type field and the manual. Many search results labelled propane generator are dual fuel units, and some propane-only listings are replacement parts, conversion kits or long-discontinued models still showing up in search. Buy the current model number, from a listing that is actually in stock.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-propane" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why Run a Generator on Propane at All
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Storage: Propane Does Not Go Stale</h3>

          <p>
            The single best reason to choose a propane generator is storage. Gasoline starts to degrade within months. Ethanol blends absorb water, and stale fuel varnishes carburettors, which is the most common reason a generator that has sat since last winter will not start when the power goes out. Propane does not go stale. A filled cylinder stored correctly outdoors is ready years later, and a generator that only ever runs on propane has no liquid fuel sitting in its carburettor to gum up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Easier to Store in Quantity</h3>

          <p>
            Propane is also easier to store in quantity. Most homes already have one or two 20 lb barbecue cylinders, exchange cages are at every gas station and hardware store, and a 100 lb cylinder delivered by a propane supplier sits outdoors on a pad. Storing 20 gallons of gasoline at home is a fire hazard and, in many places, against local fire codes. Storing five or six propane cylinders outside is routine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cleaner Burning, No Spills</h3>

          <p>
            Propane burns cleaner, which means less carbon build-up and, owners commonly report, cleaner oil between changes. It does not spill when you refuel; you swap a cylinder and open the valve. And there is no fuel to carry in the car to a campsite if you already bring propane for a stove.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Case Against: Less Energy</h3>

          <p>
            The case against is mostly about energy. Propane carries less energy per gallon than gasoline, so a generator makes less power on it and burns more of it by volume. In very cold weather a small cylinder can struggle to supply vapour fast enough. Both problems are manageable, but they are real, and they are why the honest answer for most households is a dual fuel generator run on propane by default, with gasoline as the backup.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="derate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Propane Derate: About 10 Percent Less Power
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Propane Makes Less Power</h3>

          <p>
            Every generator makes less power on propane than on gasoline, because a gallon of propane carries roughly 91,500 BTU against around 114,000 for gasoline, and engines are tuned primarily for gasoline. The spec sheets on this page show the size of the gap. The Westinghouse iGen2550DFc drops from 1,900 to 1,700 running watts on propane. The iGen5000DFc drops from 3,900 to 3,500. The WGen9500DF drops from 9,500 to 8,500. The Champion 200988 drops from 3,500 to about 3,150. In every case, that is roughly a 10 percent cut.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Size on the Propane Figure</h3>

          <p>
            For sizing, use the propane figure, not the headline gasoline number. If a box says 9,500 watts but you plan to run it on propane, plan on 8,500. If you are sizing for a well pump or a central air conditioner, a 10 percent shortfall is the difference between starting it and tripping the breaker. Our <a href="/what-size-generator-do-i-need-for-my-house/">what size generator do I need for my house</a> guide walks through the sizing, and the propane derate simply comes off the end of that calculation.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watch for Gasoline-Only Ratings</h3>

          <p>
            Be wary of listings that only publish gasoline ratings for a dual fuel unit. Reputable manufacturers publish both, usually in a small table. If you cannot find the propane figure, assume roughly 10 percent less on running watts and look harder before buying. Starting watts are derated too, which matters for motor loads such as pumps and compressors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Altitude Stacks on Top</h3>

          <p>
            Altitude stacks on top of the derate. Engines lose output as the air thins, commonly estimated at roughly 3 to 3.5 percent per 1,000 feet above sea level. A propane generator at 5,000 feet can be down meaningfully compared with its sea-level gasoline rating. If you live high, size up.
          </p>

