        <div id="tank-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. 20 lb Tank Runtime Math
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a 20 lb Cylinder Holds</h3>

          <p>
            A 20 lb cylinder, the standard barbecue tank, holds about 4.6 to 4.7 gallons of propane when it is filled correctly. That works out to roughly 430,000 BTU, which is about the energy in 3.7 gallons of gasoline. Because propane carries less energy by volume, a generator's propane runtime on a 20 lb cylinder is usually shorter than people expect.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Runtime From the Spec Sheets</h3>

          <p>
            Here is how the numbers play out with the spec sheets on this page. Westinghouse lists the large WGen9500DF at up to 7 hours on a 20 lb cylinder. Our <a href="/how-much-propane-does-a-generator-use/">how much propane does a generator use</a> guide puts a mid-size portable at roughly five to eight hours per 20 lb cylinder at moderate load, and a small inverter at eight to twelve hours. Heavy loads cut those figures sharply; light loads stretch them, and inverters stretch them most because their engines slow down at light load.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Planning an Outage</h3>

          <p>
            To plan an outage, multiply. Suppose you run a mid-size propane generator twelve hours a day for three days and get about six hours per cylinder. That is 36 hours of running and six 20 lb cylinders. One spare tank in the garage covers one evening, not a weekend. For anything more than a short outage, the practical answer is a 100 lb cylinder, which holds about 23 gallons, or a connection to a larger permanent tank with the correct regulator and hose.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Practical Notes on Filling</h3>

          <p>
            A few practical notes. Tanks are filled to about 80 percent of capacity to allow for expansion, so do not count on more than about 4.7 gallons from a 20 lb cylinder. Exchange cylinders are often filled to only 15 lb, a quarter less than a refill. And a hose that lets you connect a larger tank, or two cylinders through a changeover valve, pays for itself the first time a tank runs dry at night.
          </p>

