        <div id="cold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cold Weather: The Propane Vapour Problem
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Cold Starves the Vapour</h3>

          <p>
            Propane sits in the tank as a liquid and the generator runs on the vapour that boils off it. That boiling needs heat, which the cylinder pulls from the surrounding air. As temperatures drop, the liquid boils off more slowly and tank pressure falls. Propane boils at about minus 44 degrees F, so it never stops vaporising in normal winter weather, but a small cylinder can struggle to keep up with a hungry engine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Happens on a Cold Night</h3>

          <p>
            In practice, that means a 20 lb cylinder feeding a large generator on a very cold night can frost over, lose pressure and starve the engine, even with propane still in the tank. Owners commonly report this with large generators below freezing. A bigger tank has more surface area and more liquid to draw heat from, so a 100 lb cylinder copes far better than a 20 lb one. Smaller generators draw less and are less affected.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Fixes</h3>

          <p>
            The fixes are straightforward. Size the tank to the generator, using a 100 lb cylinder or larger for anything above roughly 5,000 watts in winter. Keep the cylinder out of the wind and off frozen ground, and never apply direct heat, open flame or an electric heater not designed for propane cylinders. Some owners use purpose-made tank blankets, which are designed for the job. And keep gasoline as a fallback, which is the strongest argument for dual fuel in cold climates.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cold Starts</h3>

          <p>
            Starting is the other cold-weather issue. All small engines are harder to start in the cold, and propane-only operation can make the first start of the morning slower. Champion advertises cold start technology on its 2,500 watt dual fuel unit for this reason. Electric start, found on the iGen5000DFc, WGen9500DF and Champion 200988, makes a cold start much less of a chore than a pull cord in the snow.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="connections" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Hoses, Regulators and Connecting a Bigger Tank
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Standard 20 lb Hose</h3>

          <p>
            Every generator on this page ships with, or is designed for, a hose that connects to a standard 20 lb cylinder. That is enough for occasional use. For anything longer, you will want to connect a larger tank, and that is where people make mistakes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Upgrading to a 100 lb Cylinder</h3>

          <p>
            A 100 lb cylinder is the easy upgrade: it stays outdoors on the ground next to the generator and usually connects with a longer hose or an adapter rated for propane, matched to the cylinder's valve type. A permanent tank, such as the 250 or 500 gallon tank that feeds a house, is a different matter. It runs at different pressure and needs a correctly sized regulator and an approved connection, normally installed by a propane supplier or a licensed technician. Do not improvise this connection.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hose Care and Leak Checks</h3>

          <p>
            Keep the hose short, rated for propane, and protected from being stepped on or driven over. Check fittings with soapy water for leaks each time you connect a tank. Store cylinders outdoors, upright, away from the generator's exhaust, and never inside a garage or basement. And when the outage is over, close the cylinder valve first and let the generator run the line dry before you shut it down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Connecting to the House</h3>

          <p>
            If you are connecting a propane generator to the house, the generator side matters as much as the propane side. Use a transfer switch or an interlock with an inlet box, never a back-feed cord through a dryer outlet. Our <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch guide</a> explains the options and costs. Generators with a 50 amp outlet, such as the WGen9500DF, connect to a 50 amp inlet; our <a href="/best-generator-with-50-amp-outlet/">best generator with a 50 amp outlet</a> guide covers that class.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Sizing a Propane Generator for Your House
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Essentials Only</h3>

          <p>
            Start from what you need to run, then take the propane derate off the generator's rating. For a refrigerator, a freezer, lights, a router and phone charging, you need about 1,500 to 2,000 running watts. A 1,700 propane running watt unit such as the iGen2550DFc covers that, as long as you do not add a microwave or space heater at the same time.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Adding a Sump Pump and Furnace Blower</h3>

          <p>
            Add a sump pump and a gas furnace blower, and you are into the 3,000 to 3,500 running watt range on propane, with a starting surge on top for the pump. That is the iGen5000DFc's territory. Add a 240 volt well pump, an electric water heater, a central air conditioner or the whole panel through a transfer switch, and you need 7,000 to 9,000 propane running watts and 240 volt outlets, which is where the WGen9500DF fits. Our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a> lists typical figures for each appliance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sizing the Fuel</h3>

          <p>
            Then size the fuel. A small inverter running essentials can manage a night on a 20 lb cylinder. A large open-frame generator running half the house needs a 100 lb cylinder for a day. If you are buying a big generator to run on propane, budget for the propane storage at the same time; a generator without fuel is a lawn ornament.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Going Further by Wattage</h3>

          <p>
            If you want a generator for a specific outlet size or wattage band, the sibling guides go further. Our <a href="/best-3500-watt-generator/">best 3500 watt generator</a> and <a href="/best-12000-watt-generator/">best 12000 watt generator</a> guides cover the classes either side of the picks here, and our <a href="/best-portable-generator/">best portable generator</a> pillar ranks the whole field.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-standby" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Portable Propane vs a Standby Generator
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Standby Generator Makes Sense</h3>

          <p>
            If you already have a large propane tank feeding your furnace or water heater, a standby generator connected to that tank is the propane powered generator that requires the least work. It starts itself when the power goes out, switches the house over through an automatic transfer switch, and runs for days from a 250 or 500 gallon tank. It is also several times the cost of a portable once installation, the transfer switch and the gas line are included.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Portable Makes Sense</h3>

          <p>
            A portable propane generator makes sense when the budget does not stretch to standby, when you rent or expect to move, or when you also want a generator for camping, tailgating or a job site. It is also the better choice if your outages are rare and short, because a standby unit's cost is hard to justify for one or two outages a year.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Middle Path</h3>

          <p>
            A middle path is a large dual fuel portable like the WGen9500DF, fed from a 100 lb cylinder, connected to the house through an inlet box and a manual transfer switch or interlock. That gets you much of a standby's capability for a fraction of the cost, at the price of wheeling it out and starting it yourself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">More Outage Planning</h3>

          <p>
            For outage planning more broadly, our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generator for power outages</a> guide compares portable, inverter and standby options, and our <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">guide to placing a generator outside</a> covers distances from doors, windows and vents. Propane does not make a generator's exhaust any safer. Carbon monoxide is just as deadly from an LP powered generator as from a gasoline one.
          </p>
        </div>

