        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are propane-only generators still sold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Very few. Generac's LP-series propane-only portables are discontinued, and when we checked, Sun Joe's SJG4100LP propane-only portable was listed as unavailable on both Amazon and Sun Joe's store. Small specialist brands sell 1,000 watt propane-only units elsewhere. Most buyers today get a dual fuel generator and run it on propane, keeping gasoline as a backup.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a generator run on a 20 lb propane tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on size and load. Westinghouse rates its large WGen9500DF at up to 7 hours per 20 lb cylinder. A mid-size portable typically gets around five to eight hours at moderate load, and a small inverter eight to twelve. Heavy loads cut those figures sharply, and exchange cylinders are often filled to only 15 lb.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a generator make less power on propane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Propane carries less energy per gallon than gasoline, so dual fuel generators are rated lower on propane, typically by about 10 percent on running watts. The Westinghouse WGen9500DF, for example, is rated at 9,500 running watts on gasoline and 8,500 on propane. Always size against the propane rating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is propane better than gasoline for a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For storage and engine cleanliness, yes. Propane does not go stale, does not varnish carburettors and is safer to store in quantity. Gasoline makes more power, is easier to find after a storm in some areas and works better in extreme cold. That is why dual fuel, run on propane by default, is the best choice for most people.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a propane generator in cold weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with planning. In freezing temperatures a small cylinder can struggle to supply vapour fast enough under heavy load, frosting over and losing pressure. Use a larger tank, such as a 100 lb cylinder, for bigger generators, keep it sheltered from wind, and never apply open flame. Electric start helps with cold starts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I connect a portable generator to my house propane tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be done, but not with improvised fittings. A permanent house tank needs the correct regulator and an approved connection, normally installed by a propane supplier or licensed technician. A 100 lb cylinder is a simpler option for most portables; it usually connects with a longer hose or a propane-rated adapter matched to its valve.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between this and a dual fuel generator guide?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Our dual fuel roundup ranks machines mainly on gasoline output and whole-home backup. This guide ranks them on propane: published propane watts, propane runtime and cold-weather behaviour. If you plan to run mostly on propane, start here. If you plan to run mostly on gasoline, the dual fuel guide is the better fit.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing on the gasoline rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The headline number on a dual fuel box is almost always the gasoline rating. On propane, expect roughly 10 percent less running power. A 9,500 watt generator is an 8,500 watt generator on LP. Size your loads, including motor starting surges, against the propane figure, and do not assume it is the same.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning an outage on one 20 lb cylinder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 20 lb cylinder holds about 4.7 gallons, which is one evening on a large generator and a night on a small inverter. Work out hours per cylinder at your load, multiply by the hours you will run, and buy the cylinders or a 100 lb tank before the storm, not during it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a big generator off a small tank in the cold</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                In freezing weather, a 20 lb cylinder can frost over and lose pressure under a heavy load, starving the engine with propane still inside. Use a 100 lb cylinder or larger for big generators in winter, keep it out of the wind, never heat it with a flame, and keep gasoline as a backup.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a propane-only listing that is not really for sale</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many propane-only generators in search results are discontinued, out of stock, or are actually conversion kits and parts. Check the current model number, the fuel type field and stock status before paying. A dual fuel unit run only on propane is usually the better buy on today's market.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Improvising a connection to a house tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 250 or 500 gallon house tank runs at different pressure from a portable cylinder and needs the correct regulator and an approved connection. Do not rig it yourself with mismatched fittings. Have a propane supplier or licensed technician make the connection, and leak-test every fitting with soapy water.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

